{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why does a time shift not change the magnitude spectrum?",say:'Question one. Why does a time shift leave the magnitude spectrum unchanged?',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why does a'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why does a time shift not change the magnitude spectrum?",say:'A time shift multiplies X of f by e to the minus j two pi f t zero, whose magnitude is one at every frequency. So the magnitude spectrum stays the same; only the phase changes.',cues:{a1:'A time shift',a1b:'whose magnitude is',a2:'So the magnitude',a2b:'only the phase'},al:[['The shift multiplies X(f) by exp(−j2πft₀), whose magnitude is 1 at every f.','a1'],['So |X(f)| stays the same; only the phase changes, by −2πft₀.','a2']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CM_T(20,34,'time: x(t) and x(t − t₀)',{fs:24,fw:700})+CM_L(20,150,600,150,CM_GRY,2)+`<polyline points="20,150 120,150 120,70 220,70 220,150 600,150" fill="none" stroke="${BLU}" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round"/>`+`<polyline points="300,150 300,70 400,70 400,150" fill="none" stroke="${ORG}" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="10 7" stroke-linejoin="round"/>`+CM_arrow(230,110,290,110,ORG,3)+CM_T(260,98,'t₀',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1b'),CM_T(20,204,'|X(f)|: the same curve for both',{fs:24,fw:700})+CM_L(20,330,600,330,CM_GRY,2)+CM_curve(t=>Math.abs(CM_sinc(t)),-3,3,40,580,330,100,BLU,5)+CM_curve(t=>Math.abs(CM_sinc(t)),-3,3,40,580,330,100,ORG,3,160,'stroke-dasharray="10 8"'))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2b'),CM_T(20,384,'phase: an extra −2πft₀',{fs:24,fw:700,fill:ORG})+CM_L(20,440,600,440,CM_GRY,2)+CM_L(310,390,310,500,CM_GRY,2)+CM_L(120,404,500,476,ORG,4)+CM_T(600,500,'f',{fs:22,a:'end',fill:CM_MUT}))}`},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A 4 kHz message is sent by AM on a 10 MHz carrier. Find the carrier wavelength, the quarter-wave antenna length and the bandwidth of the modulated signal. Take c = 3×10⁸ m/s.",say:`Question two. A four kilohertz message goes by ${P('AM')} on a ten megahertz carrier. Find the carrier wavelength, the quarter-wave antenna and the bandwidth.`,cues:{k:'Question two',q:'A four kilohertz'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"A 4 kHz message is sent by AM on a 10 MHz carrier. Find the carrier wavelength, the quarter-wave antenna length and the bandwidth of the modulated signal. Take c = 3×10⁸ m/s.",say:`Lambda is three times ten to the eight over ten to the seven: thirty meters. A quarter is seven point five meters. ${P('AM')} takes twice the message bandwidth: eight kilohertz, nine point nine nine six to ten point zero zero four megahertz.`,cues:{fk:'Lambda is three',k1:'over ten to',k1b:'thirty meters',k2:'A quarter is',k2b:'seven point five',k3:'takes twice the',k3b:'kilohertz, nine point'},calc:{f:[`λ = c / ${CM_FC},  ${CM_BS} = 2B`],hint:`c = 3×10⁸ m/s; ${CM_FC} = 10 MHz = 10⁷ Hz.`,fk:'fk',steps:[["Wavelength",["λ = 3×10⁸ ÷ 1×10⁷","λ = 30 m"],["k1","k1b"]],["Quarter-wave",["λ/4 = 30 m ÷ 4","λ/4 = 7.5 m"],["k2","k2b"]],["Bandwidth",[`${CM_BS} = 2 × 4 kHz = 8 kHz`,`${CM_BS} = 8 kHz: 9.996 to 10.004 MHz`],["k3","k3b"]]],live:[0,1,2]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Two stations in one city each carry a 5 kHz audio message by AM. Explain why they cannot both use a 1 MHz carrier, and find the smallest carrier spacing.",say:`Question three. Two stations in one city each send a five kilohertz message by ${P('AM')}. Why can't both use one megahertz, and what is the smallest carrier spacing?`,cues:{k:'Question three',q:'Two stations in'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Two stations in one city each carry a 5 kHz audio message by AM. Explain why they cannot both use a 1 MHz carrier, and find the smallest carrier spacing.",say:`Each ${P('AM')} signal fills ten kilohertz around its carrier. On one carrier the spectra overlap, and no filter can separate them. Each reaches five kilohertz either side, so the carriers need ten kilohertz between them; more, for a guard band.`,cues:{a1:'Each AM signal',a2:'On one carrier',a3:'Each reaches five'},al:[["Each AM signal occupies 2B = 10 kHz: 0.995 to 1.005 MHz.",'a1'],["On one carrier the spectra overlap, so no filter can separate them.",'a2'],["Each reaches 5 kHz either side: carriers at least 5 + 5 = 10 kHz apart (more, for a guard band).",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>{const xf=f=>30+(f-0.985)*14000,hp=(f0,y,col,op,ex='')=>CM_area(CM_hump,-1,1,xf(f0-0.005),xf(f0+0.005),y,90,col,op,60,ex),ax=(y,ticks)=>CM_L(20,y,600,y,CM_GRY,2)+ticks.map(f=>CM_L(xf(f),y,xf(f),y+8,CM_MUT,2)+CM_T(xf(f),y+32,f.toFixed(3),{fs:22,a:'middle',fill:CM_MUT})).join('');
return g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CM_T(20,36,'both on 1.000 MHz',{fs:24,fw:700})+ax(190,[0.995,1.000,1.005])+hp(1.000,190,BLU,.25)+hp(1.000,190,ORG,.18,'stroke-dasharray="10 7"'))
+g('pop',tk(c,'a2')+.6,CM_X(xf(1.000),140,24,TRED)+CM_T(470,110,'overlap',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:TRED}))
+g('pop',tk(c,'a3'),CM_T(20,286,'carriers 10 kHz apart (MHz)',{fs:24,fw:700})+ax(440,[0.995,1.005,1.015])+hp(1.000,440,BLU,.25)+hp(1.010,440,ORG,.25)+CM_brk(xf(1.000),xf(1.010),328,GRN,-12)+CM_T((xf(1.000)+xf(1.010))/2,318,'10 kHz',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN}))}},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes are the PDF linked in the description. Redraw the five-block chain and the two pulse spectra from memory, then check them.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the five-block'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["The two chapters", "Elements of a communication system; need for modulation; Fourier transform review and band-pass signals"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["The communication chain", "Five blocks with noise entering at the channel; below, the quarter-wave antenna on a log scale: 75 km at 1 kHz, 0.75 m at 100 MHz."], ["The pulse and its two spectra", "The 2 V, 1 ms pulse: a sinc of peak 2 mV/Hz with nulls at ±1 kHz. On a 10 kHz carrier: copies of 1 mV/Hz filling 9 to 11 kHz."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: B. P. Lathi and Zhi Ding (Modern Digital and Analog Communication Systems); Simon Haykin and Michael Moher (Communication Systems)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:'So remember: noise joins at the channel, the carrier makes the antenna practical, and a real carrier gives two half-height copies. Next time, the Hilbert transform and complex envelope. See you there!',cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'noise joins at',w2:'the carrier makes',w3:'a real carrier gives',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['NOISE JOINS AT THE CHANNEL','BLU','w1'],['A CARRIER SHRINKS THE ANTENNA','ORG','w2'],['TWO HALF-HEIGHT COPIES','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Hilbert transform and complex envelope", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
